# -- end of source
# f1 = add, f2 = line, f3 = tag, f4 = overOne, f5 = overTwo, f6 = overText, f7 = overParameter, f8 = attempt
# `add.bind(null, 4)` at the top of a file, called in a loop of another function. Of the word of the bound arguments Link kept a mark that there are some; the walk knew their number only where the bind stands in the same body.
# Where the text of the bind tells how many arguments it binds, the type of what it makes says the number: BoundFunction<add, never, 1>: and the call asks the cell for it.
# The primitives that are bound are seen to arrive at the parameters of the target (objects are not followed there): the call asks the words of the cell for them, so that the copy of the target reckons with numbers.
# Nothing of it is proven: whatever else stands there is called as written.
# RUN(HELD): link held variables mechanisms=all
# HELD: BoundFunction<f1, never, 1>
# HELD: BoundFunction<f3, never, 1>
# HELD: BoundFunction<f2, never, 2>
# RUN(ONE): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# ONE: = HasNumberOfBoundArguments {{*}}count=1
# ONE: = GetWordOfBoundFunction {{*}}index=2
# ONE: = Add {{*}}form=Integers
# RUN(TWO): walk f5 mechanisms=all
# TWO: = HasNumberOfBoundArguments {{*}}count=2
# TWO: = GetWordOfBoundFunction {{*}}index=3
# RUN(TEXTS): walk f6 mechanisms=all
# TEXTS: = HasNumberOfBoundArguments {{*}}count=1
# RUN(HANDED): walk f7 mechanisms=all
# HANDED: = HasNumberOfBoundArguments {{*}}count=2
# HANDED: = TypeTest {{*}}testedType=i32
# The shell binds whatever it likes: other numbers, fewer, none, more, a fraction, a text, an object, undefined, a BigInt where whole numbers were seen; twice; another target; no bound function at all.
